{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1529551-how-would-werewolves-that-still-keep-their-old-religion-syncretized-them-together","id":1529551,"title":"How would werewolves that still keep their old religion syncretized them together ?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2024-01-27T07:46:23-08:00","id":1529551,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> How would werewolves that still keep their old religion syncretized them together ?","content":"I have been working in several characters that are affiliated with a religion but turn into werewolves but despite that they keep their old religions and tried to reconcile the two realities they now face, but its hard to come with a valid justification","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26408-luchya","name":"Luchya","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-27T08:02:01-08:00","id":1529552,"title":"","content":"There are multiple instances across the books of characters doing this; what particular difficulties are you running into?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-27T08:48:02-08:00","id":1529554,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1529551-how-would-werewolves-that-still-keep-their-old-religion-syncretized-them-together?p=1529552#post1529552\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There are multiple instances across the books of characters doing this; what particular difficulties are you running into?</div> </div> </div> </div>Well i have a characte who is devoted jew and i find it particularly difficult to like come up with a reason as to why or even how they keep their old faith while being awoken to this new reality of the world, like i know the lodge of hasan exist but that just deal with spirits not with like mother wolf and father luna and the hole mythology surrounding the uratha lore. This is probably because i dont know much about judais probably wich i should have started from there i guess sorry","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26408-luchya","name":"Luchya","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-27T14:46:59-08:00","id":1529569,"title":"","content":"The practical concerns for any sort of werewolf approach to religion are going to start with where along the axes of available information and belief the characters are starting with.<br /> <br /> To begin with, has the character been exposed to the wider shared culture of the People (and if so, were they given the Forsaken or Pure take on things)?<br /> <br /> Separately, while presumably the mention of trying to reconcile the two realities they face tilts the answer more in one direction than the other, the question of how much the character is willing to discard or change from their previous belief system (as opposed to rigidly adhering to elements of it that flatly clash with their new conditions) is worth addressing.<br /> <br /> The amount of werewolf and spiritual activity in the area is also going to influence things, as is the strength of the local Gauntlet and how much and what kind of social support the character has access to (e.g. a pack of Ghost Wolves helping you out looks different from a well-meaning chapter of Long Night hunters seeking to cure you of demonic influence).<br /> <br /> From there, the prospects of fitting the newer information into the older belief system and accounting for their becoming an obligate carnivore with rage issues who can smell Concepts Made Flesh From Another World is mostly a matter of how you want to build that part of your setting. Depending on how exposed to the nonhuman mythology they are, Urfarah might never come up or they might be the Devil or they might be the primal face of God.<br /> <br /> At the top level of design, it's important to have an idea of why you're including a story element in your setting so that you can steer in that direction. Obviously being a devout adherent to one's faith before the trauma of the First Change is a ready reason to try to cling to something significant and familiar, but knowing what you want that to mean for the rest of their screentime in the game will help determine whether this is a story about a character's strong faith changing into something beyond recognition, a story about unbending devotion to a human faith slowly destroying their ability to function as something no longer human, a story about trying to reconcile the human and the inhuman, an examination of the significance of the tenets of one's faith when taken outside its normal context, or something else.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-27T22:41:46-08:00","id":1529583,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26408-luchya\" style=\"background-image:url('data:image/svg+xml;base64,');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"26408\" data-vbnamecard=\"26408\">Luchya</a> remember that alternative Uratha myths are a thing - and even 2E corebook do not convey it greatly ( due to limited word count ), in 1E we had few different takes on werewolf cosmology. More to it, 7 years ago ( how old I'm! ) on this very forum we pointed to few takes on this story. I myself proposed 3 of them:<br /> * Norse / Scandinavian / Vikings werewolf creation myth<br /> * Ancient Romans wolf creation myth, and, most interesting to us....<br /> * <i><b>Jewish werewolf creations myth</b></i> <b>\ud83d\ude0e</b><br /> Here is jist of it - <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/137256-alternative-werewolves-myths?p=298515#post298515\"><b>more you can read in this topic.</b></a><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>wyrdhamster</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/137256-alternative-werewolves-myths?p=298515#post298515\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><b>Unholy Sons</b><br /> On the beginning was a Word. And Word was a World. The Father created with the Word whole Creation. And it was good. He later created humans in Paradise and gave them free will. But Eve, first women, was tempted by the Snake to obtain the apple from Tree of Knowledge. And they needed to be banished from the Paradise.<br /> <br /> But Father weren\u2019t only having righteous face of the Sun. He was forgiving as the Moon. He couldn\u2019t lifted his own decree, because he knew it was right - humans weren\u2019t suppose to return to Paradise any more. But he also couldn\u2019t left Adam and Eve, and the kind alone in the world. He needed to defend them, and Paradise against them.<br /> <br /> As the Moon, he appeared to the most dangerous of Paradise animal and come to make with it the race of guardians. The Father Moon conceived with Mother Wolf new kind - half man, half wolves. Not pure as humans, but needed to protect them and the world from the man. As sons and daughters of so foul creature as Wolf, they could do what need to be done - even go to the Hell itself, if the need rose.<br /> <br /> But the Wolf Mother wanted more and more of her half human children to behave like a wolves, forsaking they sacred duty from the Father. Then he come to them and told what need to be done. And so they killed they Mother under his command, like Isaac was ready to sacrifice his son.<br /> <br /> This is who we are - we are His children, with duty to him and respect to our Mother memory. We didn\u2019t destroyed the Paradise, but were made to guard it. And now we atone for our sins in the eyes of Father.</div> </div> </div> </div>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2024-01-29T15:44:32-08:00","id":1529670,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1437_5.jpg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1437\" data-vbnamecard=\"1437\">wyrdhamster</a> nice one.<br /> <br /> Can I propose another suggestion, a non-religious member of a religious community/ethnicity (like Irish Catholic, raised in the culture but never believing... Cause I feel more comfortable talking about my own culture than trying to shoe-horn the idea into anyone else's) who starts to believe and research their faith only after becoming a werewolf.<br /> <br /> Like, sure they never really believed in all that Angels and Demons stuff, but now that they know there are Werewolves and Spirits out there, they have a newly awakened faith... They believe they have only discovered the tip of the iceberg, and the question is how skeptical they are of the werewolf creation myths they are now being offered.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27248-orathaic","name":"orathaic","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar27248_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1032"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}